{"duration_api_ms":76617,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"4e041681-475c-4797-b3a8-49b11786ff19","total_cost_usd":0.3617790000000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19769,"cache_read_input_tokens":101335,"output_tokens":9166,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":509},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19769,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1034,"cache_read_input_tokens":29857,"cache_creation_input_tokens":746,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":746},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":10,"outputTokens":9166,"cacheReadInputTokens":101335,"cacheCreationInputTokens":19769,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3617790000000001,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":509,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":5,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I wrote the suite in `tests/test_agent.py`, but it doesn't fully pass: **107 tests pass and 3 fail.** The 3 failures come from a real bug in the library, not from mistakes in the tests, so I left them failing rather than weakening them.\n\n**The bug:** RFC 6901 says an array index is either `0` or a number with no leading zero (`%x30 / ( %x31-39 *(%x30-39) )`). The library checks indices with the pattern `[0-9]+$` at `jsonpointer.py:155`, so it accepts `01`, `00` and `007`. For example, `resolve_pointer(list(range(10)), \"/01\")` returns `1` when it should raise `JsonPointerException`. The comment right above that line says leading zeros must not be allowed, so the code doesn't do what it says. The failing tests are the three cases of `test_leading_zero_indices_rejected`.\n\n**What the suite covers:**\n- **RFC examples:** every example from section 5 of the spec, plus checking that the empty pointer returns the whole document itself.\n- **Resolving:** nested lookups; `%20` isn't treated as URL encoding; missing keys, out-of-range indices and invalid indices (`-1`, `+1`, `1.0`, spaces, empty) raise errors; defaults are returned on failure, including falsy defaults like `None`, `0` and `\"\"`; `-` means \"end of list\" for lists but is a normal key for dicts; numeric keys in dicts stay strings; lookups into strings or plain values fail; objects with their own `__getitem__` work; `VERBOSE_EXCEPTIONS` controls whether the document appears in error messages.\n- **Parsing:** pointers must start with `/`; bad escapes (`~`, `~2`, `~x`) are rejected; `~01` unescapes to `~1`, not `/`; escaping and unescaping round-trip.\n- **Pointer objects:** `path`, `str` and `repr` round-trip (including subclasses); equality and hashing; `\"\"` and `\"/\"` are different pointers.\n- **Building pointers:** `from_parts`, plus `join` and `/` with a string, a pointer or a list. Joining escapes special characters, leaves the original pointer unchanged, and raises on invalid suffixes.\n- **Containment:** `contains` and `in` compare whole parts, so `/ab` doesn't count as containing `/a`.\n- **Setting values:** changing in place vs. on a deep copy; setting the root; replacing a list item and appending with `-`; failures for out-of-range indices, strings, tuples, plain values and missing parent paths.\n- **Internal helpers:** `to_last`, `get_part`, `walk` and `pairwise`.\n\nIf you want the run to go green before the library is fixed, I can mark that one test as an expected failure (`xfail(strict=True)`). It will then start failing loudly once the bug is fixed, as a reminder to remove the marker.","ttft_ms":2334,"type":"result","duration_ms":77469,"uuid":"e9779918-f831-43ec-a407-c731e392b7f4","ttft_stream_ms":1900,"time_to_request_ms":205,"first_content_frame_ms":1901,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
